\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Problem Solving Techniques\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Start here for systematic approaches to complex problems}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PREPARE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Prioritize the problem  \line
R - Research & brainstorm solutions  \line
E - Evaluate available options  \line
P - Plan steps to resolve issue  \line
A - Act on the plan  \line
R - Reflect on results  \line
E - Evaluate and revise plan as necessary  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Strategic planning sessions (1-2 hours) - Medium complexity problems with multiple stakeholders - When you need structured documentation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Analysis paralysis} - Spending 2 hours planning a 10-minute problem. If you\u8217're past 30 minutes on \u8220"Research\u8221", switch to IDEA. - {\b Skipping reflection} - Acting without the final \u8220"Reflect/Evaluate\u8221" creates recurring issues. Always close the loop. - {\b Wrong tool for crisis} - Don\u8217't PREPARE during a production outage. Use STOP \u8594 ? TRACE \u8594 ? DEBUG first, PREPARE during post-mortem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} RACI (assign responsibilities), SWOT (evaluate options), ICEBERG (if reflection reveals deeper issues)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Planning infrastructure migration - Use PREPARE to structure approach, RACI during planning phase for role clarity, SWOT to evaluate cloud provider options}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PADDER\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Pinpoint problem  \line
A - Analyze data and look for patterns  \line
D - Develop solution & consider other ways to solve the issue - try to have more than one   \line
D - Design action plan  \line
E - Execute action plan & Monitor Results  \line
R - Reevaluate and refine plan as needed  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Data-driven problem solving - When patterns need to be identified - Pairs with 8D Approach\u8217's D3 (Interim Containment) for quick fixes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} IDEA (simpler version), 8D Approach (formal resolution)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Recurring server crashes - Pinpoint timing, Analyze logs for patterns, Develop interim solutions (restart service) + permanent fix (increase memory), Monitor effectiveness}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 ICEBERG\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 I - Identify issue(s)  \line
C - Collect data and analyze situation  \line
E - Examine possible (root) causes  \line
B - Brainstorms solutions  \line
E - Execute solution(s)  \line
R - Review, evaluate, and adjust solutions  \line
G - Gather feedback  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Complex problems requiring deep analysis (30-60 min) - When surface symptoms hide deeper root causes - Escalate from IDEA when complexity increases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Going too deep on simple problems} - Using ICEBERG for a 5-minute password reset. Start with IDEA, escalate only if complexity emerges. - {\b Skipping feedback (G)} - Gathering feedback seems optional but prevents recurrence. Always complete the full cycle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} 5 Whys (deeper root cause), 8D Approach (formal prevention), IDEA (start simple, escalate if needed)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Network performance degradation - Identify slowness, Collect metrics (latency, packet loss), Examine causes (routing changes, bandwidth saturation), Brainstorm solutions, Execute, Review with team, Gather feedback from users}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 IDEA\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 I - Identify problem  \line
D - Develop Solution  \line
E - Execute Solution   \line
A - Assess Solution  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Quick, simple problems (2-5 minutes) - Crisis situations requiring immediate action - Use STOP first if under stress\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Escalation failure} - Not moving to ICEBERG or PREPARE when problem reveals complexity. If you\u8217're past 10 minutes or hitting multiple blockers, escalate. - {\b Skipping assessment} - Executing without validating the solution worked. The \u8220"A\u8221" step prevents recurring issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} STOP (crisis stress management), PREPARE/ICEBERG (escalation path for complex issues)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i User can\u8217't access VPN - Identify (connection error), Develop (reset credentials), Execute (send new password), Assess (confirm access restored)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5 Whys\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Keep asking why till root causes are identified\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Essential for root cause analysis - During 8D Approach\u8217's D4 (Root Cause Analysis) - Combine with ICEBERG for systematic deep dives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Stopping at symptoms} - Stopping at \u8220"Why #3: Database slow\u8221" instead of drilling to root cause (missing migration). Keep asking until you reach a process/systemic failure. - {\b Assuming single root cause} - Complex problems often have multiple root causes. Use 5 Whys for each branch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} ICEBERG (structured approach), 8D Approach (formal incident management)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Deployment failures - Why? Pipeline failed. Why? Tests timed out. Why? Database slow. Why? Index missing. Why? Schema change didn\u8217't include migration. Root cause: Missing migration validation step}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 8D Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Industry standard in automotive/manufacturing, adapted for IT incident management}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 D1 - Form a team  \line
D2 - Describe the problem  \line
D3 - Interim Containment Action (the "band-aid")  \line
D4 - Root Cause Analysis & Escape Point(s)  \line
D5 - Permanent Corrective Actions  \line
D6 - Implement & Validate Corrective Actions  \line
D7 - Prevent reoccurrence(s)  \line
D8 - Closure & Celebration  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Critical incidents requiring formal resolution - Problems needing documentation and prevention - When team coordination is essential (D1: use RACI)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Stopping at D3} - Implementing the band-aid but never reaching D7 (Prevent Reoccurrence) means it will happen again. - {\b Solo 8D} - Trying to do all 8 steps alone instead of D1 (Form a team). Use RACI during D1 to clarify roles. - {\b Bureaucracy creep} - Over-formalizing 8D for simple problems. Use IDEA or PREPARE for non-critical issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} PADDER (D3 interim fixes), 5 Whys + ICEBERG (D4 root cause), RACI (D1 team formation)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Data breach incident - Form security response team (RACI roles), Describe scope, Contain (disable compromised accounts), Analyze root cause (5 Whys: phishing \u8594 ? no MFA \u8594 ? insufficient training), Implement MFA, Validate with penetration test, Prevent (mandatory security awareness), Celebrate team response}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5Ps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Poor planning produces pitiful products\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Problem Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Frameworks for understanding scope and impact}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RACI\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Used to identify the roles and responsibilities of different stakeholders in a problem-solving process\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 R - Responsible (does the work)\line
A - Accountable (final approval)\line
C - Consulted (provides input)\line
I - Informed (kept updated)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Resolving role confusion in teams - 8D Approach\u8217's D1 (Form a team) step - End of WAIT \u8594 ? BREATHE \u8594 ? PAUSE chain for conflict resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Multiple Accountables} - More than one \u8220"A\u8221" creates confusion. There should be exactly one Accountable person per task. - {\b Too many Consulted} - Adding everyone as \u8220"C\u8221" slows decisions. Be selective - only include those with critical input.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Infrastructure upgrade project - Responsible: DevOps engineers, Accountable: Infrastructure Manager, Consulted: Security team, Informed: All developers}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PESTEL\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Used to identify and analyze the external factors that may impact a problem or decision\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Political\line
E - Economic\line
S - Sociocultural\line
T - Technological\line
E - Environmental\line
L - Legal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Strategic planning external factor analysis - SCALE infrastructure design validation - Architecture reviews considering compliance/regulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Cloud migration planning - Political (vendor lock-in concerns), Economic (cost optimization), Technological (API compatibility), Legal (data sovereignty requirements)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SWOT\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Used to identify and analyze the internal and external factors that may impact a problem or decision\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Strengths\line
W - Weaknesses\line
O - Opportunities\line
T - Threats\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - PREPARE\u8217's \u8220"Evaluate options\u8221" step - SCALE design validation - Strategic decision making (1-2 hours)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Choosing deployment strategy - Strengths: automated rollback, Weaknesses: longer deployment time, Opportunities: canary testing, Threats: increased complexity}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SET (Systems Engineering Triangle)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Also called \u8220"Project Management Triangle\u8221" or \u8220"Iron Triangle\u8221"\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 1. Draw a triangle\line
2. Put one of these 3 words at each corner: "good", "fast", "cheap"\line
3. Pick 2 of them\line
4. The other word is what the solution will not likely be\line
   - A fast, cheap solution will not likely be good\line
   - A fast and good solution will not likely be cheap \line
   - A good and cheap solution will not likely be fast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Setting stakeholder expectations - End of SCALE \u8594 ? SWOT \u8594 ? PESTEL chain - Architecture trade-off discussions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Urgent security patch needed - Choose: Good + Fast = Not Cheap (overtime, additional resources). Manage expectations with leadership accordingly}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i From the SeBok - Systems Engineering Body of Knowledge}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Problem Resolution Threats\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Identify blockers before they derail your solution}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 DICE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 D - Delay\line
I - Incompetence\line
C - Conflict\line
E - External factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Part of IDEA \u8594 ? DICE \u8594 ? FATE rapid triage chain - Identifying blockers before execution - Risk assessment in PREPARE phase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 FATE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 F - Funding\line
A - Allocation of resources \line
T - Time\line
E - Expertise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Resource validation in rapid triage - Feasibility assessment (10-30 min) - After DICE to validate resource availability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PEST\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Political\line
E - Economic\line
S - Social\line
T - Technological\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Identifying external threats to solutions - Consider ways to combat/remove these threats\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Communication & Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Stay calm and productive during difficult conversations}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 BREATHE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 B - Breathe deeply and slowly   \line
R - Remain rational and listen  \line
E - Empathize with the other person's problem\line
A - Ask questions to understand  \line
T - Take a break if needed  \line
H - Hold back from reacting  \line
E - Express yourself calmly  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - First step when tensions rise (breathing regulates emotions) - Part of WAIT \u8594 ? BREATHE \u8594 ? PAUSE \u8594 ? RACI conflict resolution chain - If break needed, escalate to PAUSE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Fake composure} - Going through motions without actually regulating emotions. The breathing must be intentional and deep. - {\b Weaponizing calm} - Using \u8220"Let\u8217's BREATHE\u8221" dismissively to avoid addressing concerns. This damages trust - use it genuinely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} WAIT (listen first), PAUSE (step away if needed), STOP (stress management)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Stakeholder disagrees with technical approach in meeting - Breathe deeply (regulate emotions), Remain rational, Empathize with their concerns, Ask clarifying questions, Take 5-minute break if tension escalates, Hold back defensive reactions, Express technical rationale calmly}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PAUSE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Put things in perspective   \line
A - Acknowledge your feelings and theirs\line
U - Understand that you don't have to act/react right away  \line
S - Step Away from the situation  \line
E - Evaluate options and plan before acting   \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - When BREATHE isn\u8217't enough - need physical separation - \u8220"Step Away\u8221" connects to WAIT philosophy - Combine with STOP for immediate stress de-escalation (5-20 min)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} BREATHE (first step), WAIT (listen before speaking), STOP (stress response)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Heated debate about architecture decision - Put in perspective (not life-or-death), Acknowledge both viewpoints have merit, Don\u8217't decide now, Step away for lunch break, Evaluate pros/cons offline, Return with structured comparison}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 WAIT\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab \u8220"Why am I troubled / talking?\u8221"\line \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Not all things need an answer or response; don\u8217't seek trouble and don\u8217't answer negativity negatively\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Ask yourself before speaking - often listening is better - First step in WAIT \u8594 ? BREATHE \u8594 ? PAUSE \u8594 ? RACI chain - Use BREATHE first to regulate, then WAIT to choose response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Passive-aggressive silence} - Using WAIT to avoid necessary communication. This isn\u8217't \u8220"don\u8217't respond\u8221" - it\u8217's \u8220"choose the right response.\u8221" - {\b Escalation avoidance} - Not speaking up when issues need addressing. WAIT helps you respond effectively, not disappear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} BREATHE (emotional regulation), PAUSE (step away technique)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Email with accusatory tone arrives - Ask \u8220"Why am I troubled?\u8221" (ego/defensiveness), \u8220"Why am I talking?\u8221" (to defend or to resolve?), Choose not to respond immediately, Use BREATHE + PAUSE, Respond later with facts, not emotion}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stress & Resilience\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Staying calm and connected during high-pressure work}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PACE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Physical activity\line
A - Avoiding unhealthy behaviors\line
C - Coping skills\line
E - Emotional awareness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - First step in PACE \u8594 ? ARIES \u8594 ? CALM \u8594 ? SHINE burnout recovery chain - Immediate stress management actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 STOP\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Step back\line
T - Take a deep breath\line
O - Observe what is happening\line
P - Pull back and put things in perspective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - First response to ANY crisis - immediate stress management (2-5 min) - Start of STOP \u8594 ? TRACE \u8594 ? DEBUG \u8594 ? 8D crisis response chain - Use with IDEA for quick problem resolution in crisis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Common pitfalls:} - {\b Going through motions} - Rushing through STOP without actually calming. The \u8220"Take a deep breath\u8221" must be intentional - pause for 3-5 seconds. - {\b Stopping at STOP} - Using STOP but not proceeding to next step (TRACE/IDEA). STOP is the foundation, not the solution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} BREATHE + PAUSE (breathing techniques), IDEA (quick problem solving)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real-world example:} {\i Production alert at 2 AM - Step back (don\u8217't panic), Take deep breath (reduce adrenaline), Observe (read alert details), Pull back perspective (assess severity before waking team), Then proceed to TRACE for diagnostics}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 ARIES\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 A - Avoid unnecessary stress\line
R - Relax and take breaks\line
I - Incorporate physical activity into your routine\line
E - Eat a healthy diet\line
S - Sleep well\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Lifestyle changes in PACE \u8594 ? ARIES \u8594 ? CALM chain - Long-term stress reduction (2-4 weeks)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 HELP\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab This mnemonic can help you remember four ways to manage stress:\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 H - Handle problems one at a time\line
E - Exercise regularly\line
L - Learn to relax\line
P - Pace yourself and set limits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 HANDLE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab This mnemonic can help you remember six stress-management strategies:\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 H - Have a positive attitude\line
A - Avoid unnecessary stress\line
N - Nurture a strong support system\line
D - Deal with problems directly \line
L - Learn to relax, \line
E - Exercise regularly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 CALM\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 C - Confidence: Believe in your abilities and strengths\line
A - Awareness: Stay conscious of your thoughts and feelings\line
L - Logic: Use rational thinking to overcome doubts\line
M - Mindfulness: Practice being present and focused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Builds long-term resilience and confidence - Confidence connects to HOPE and SHINE (both build positive self-image) - Use after PACE or ARIES for comprehensive stress management\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} Positivity (HOPE, SHINE), Stress Management (PACE, ARIES)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PUSH\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Practice Gratitude   \line
U - Use positive affirmations  \line
S - Spend time with positive people  \line
H - Have a positive outlook  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 HOPE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 H - Harness positive thoughts  \line
O - Open up to others   \line
P - Prioritize self-care  \line
E - Exercise regularly  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SHINE\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Stay present, in the moment  \line
H - Have a healthy positive perspective  \line
I - Identify and do positive activities   \line
N - Nourish positive relationships  \line
E - Express yourself  \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - End of PACE \u8594 ? ARIES \u8594 ? CALM \u8594 ? SHINE burnout recovery chain - Ongoing practice for sustainable positivity - Connects to CALM for building positive self-image\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infrastructure & Systems Engineering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Technical mnemonics for infrastructure and DevOps work}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 TRACE (Network Troubleshooting)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 T - Test connectivity (ping, traceroute)\line
R - Review logs and metrics\line
A - Analyze packet captures\line
C - Check configurations\line
E - Escalate with documented evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Use with PREPARE and 8D Approach for systematic resolution - Start with T (Test) for quick diagnostics, escalate to A (Analyze) for deep dives - Document findings at each step for 8D Approach\u8217's D2 (Describe the problem)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} Problem Solving (PREPARE, 8D Approach)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SCALE (Infrastructure Design)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Security by design\line
C - Capacity planning\line
A - Automation-first\line
L - Load balancing\line
E - Error handling/resilience\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Use SWOT analysis alongside for design validation - Apply PESTEL to evaluate external factors affecting infrastructure - Consider SET Triangle trade-offs (fast/cheap/good) for each component\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} Problem Analysis (SWOT, PESTEL, SET)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 DEBUG (Code & System Analysis)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 D - Define the problem (what changed?)\line
E - Examine error messages/logs\line
B - Break down into components\line
U - Understand data flow\line
G - Generate hypothesis and test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} - Combines 5 Whys questioning with ICEBERG\u8217's systematic approach - Start with D (Define) - often the problem is unclear or misstated - Use with TRACE for network/system issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Combines well with:} Problem Solving (5 Whys, ICEBERG), Infrastructure (TRACE)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Proven Mnemonic Pipelines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i When certain mnemonics are combined in sequence, they create powerful workflows that amplify effectiveness. These chains are battle-tested for specific high-pressure scenarios.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1. CRISIS RESPONSE CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b STOP \u8594 ? TRACE \u8594 ? DEBUG \u8594 ? 8D}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Production outages, system failures, critical incidents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Step back (STOP)\line
T - Take a deep breath (STOP)\line
O - Observe what is happening (STOP)\line
P - Pull back and put things in perspective (STOP)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
T - Test connectivity (TRACE)\line
R - Review logs and metrics (TRACE)\line
A - Analyze packet captures (TRACE)\line
C - Check configurations (TRACE)\line
E - Escalate with documented evidence (TRACE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
D - Define the problem (DEBUG)\line
E - Examine error messages/logs (DEBUG)\line
B - Break down into components (DEBUG)\line
U - Understand data flow (DEBUG)\line
G - Generate hypothesis and test (DEBUG)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
D1-D8 - Full 8D resolution cycle (8D Approach)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} STOP stabilizes you emotionally, TRACE gives you diagnostic data, DEBUG structures your analysis, 8D ensures you prevent recurrence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} 1-4 hours (depending on complexity)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2. CONFLICT RESOLUTION CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b WAIT \u8594 ? BREATHE \u8594 ? PAUSE \u8594 ? RACI}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Team disagreements, tense meetings, stakeholder conflicts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 W - "Why am I talking?" (WAIT - listen first)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
B - Breathe deeply and slowly (BREATHE)\line
R - Remain rational and listen (BREATHE)\line
E - Empathize with the other person's problem (BREATHE)\line
A - Ask questions to understand (BREATHE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
P - Put things in perspective (PAUSE)\line
A - Acknowledge your feelings and theirs (PAUSE)\line
U - Understand you don't have to react immediately (PAUSE)\line
S - Step Away from the situation (PAUSE)\line
E - Evaluate options and plan (PAUSE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
R - Responsible (RACI - clarify roles)\line
A - Accountable (RACI)\line
C - Consulted (RACI)\line
I - Informed (RACI)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} WAIT prevents escalation, BREATHE regulates emotions, PAUSE creates space for rational thought, RACI resolves role confusion (often the root cause).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} 5-20 minutes\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3. ROOT CAUSE INVESTIGATION CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b ICEBERG \u8594 ? 5 Whys \u8594 ? PADDER \u8594 ? RACI}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Recurring issues, complex system problems, post-incident analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 I - Identify issue(s) (ICEBERG)\line
C - Collect data and analyze situation (ICEBERG)\line
E - Examine possible root causes (ICEBERG)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Why #1 \u8594 ? Why #2 \u8594 ? Why #3 \u8594 ? Why #4 \u8594 ? Why #5 (5 Whys)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
D - Develop solution (PADDER)\line
D - Design action plan (PADDER)\line
E - Execute action plan & Monitor (PADDER)\line
R - Reevaluate and refine (PADDER)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
R/A/C/I - Assign responsibilities (RACI)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} ICEBERG provides structure, 5 Whys drills to root cause, PADDER creates action plan, RACI ensures accountability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} 1-2 hours\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4. STRATEGIC DESIGN CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b SCALE \u8594 ? SWOT \u8594 ? PESTEL \u8594 ? SET}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Infrastructure planning, architecture reviews, capacity planning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 S - Security by design (SCALE)\line
C - Capacity planning (SCALE)\line
A - Automation-first (SCALE)\line
L - Load balancing (SCALE)\line
E - Error handling/resilience (SCALE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
S - Strengths (SWOT - evaluate design)\line
W - Weaknesses (SWOT)\line
O - Opportunities (SWOT)\line
T - Threats (SWOT)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
P - Political (PESTEL - external factors)\line
E - Economic (PESTEL)\line
S - Sociocultural (PESTEL)\line
T - Technological (PESTEL)\line
E - Environmental (PESTEL)\line
L - Legal (PESTEL)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
Pick 2: Good / Fast / Cheap (SET Triangle - set expectations)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} SCALE sets technical requirements, SWOT evaluates approach, PESTEL identifies external risks, SET manages stakeholder expectations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} 2-4 hours (planning phase)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5. STRESS BURNOUT RECOVERY CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b PACE \u8594 ? ARIES \u8594 ? CALM \u8594 ? SHINE}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Long-term stress, approaching burnout, need for lifestyle reset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 P - Physical activity (PACE - immediate actions)\line
A - Avoiding unhealthy behaviors (PACE)\line
C - Coping skills (PACE)\line
E - Emotional awareness (PACE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
A - Avoid unnecessary stress (ARIES - lifestyle changes)\line
R - Relax and take breaks (ARIES)\line
I - Incorporate physical activity (ARIES)\line
E - Eat a healthy diet (ARIES)\line
S - Sleep well (ARIES)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
C - Confidence (CALM - mental framework)\line
A - Awareness (CALM)\line
L - Logic (CALM)\line
M - Mindfulness (CALM)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
S - Stay present (SHINE - ongoing practice)\line
H - Have healthy perspective (SHINE)\line
I - Identify positive activities (SHINE)\line
N - Nourish relationships (SHINE)\line
E - Express yourself (SHINE)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} PACE handles immediate stress, ARIES addresses root lifestyle causes, CALM builds mental resilience, SHINE creates sustainable positivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} Ongoing (2-4 weeks for habit formation)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 6. RAPID TRIAGE CHAIN\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b IDEA \u8594 ? DICE \u8594 ? FATE}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b When to use:} Quick wins needed, time-critical decisions, assessing feasibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 I - Identify problem (IDEA)\line
D - Develop Solution (IDEA)\line
E - Execute Solution (IDEA)\line
A - Assess Solution (IDEA)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
D - Delay (DICE - check for blockers)\line
I - Incompetence (DICE)\line
C - Conflict (DICE)\line
E - External factors (DICE)\line
    \u8595 ?\line
F - Funding (FATE - resource check)\line
A - Allocation of resources (FATE)\line
T - Time (FATE)\line
E - Expertise (FATE)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why it works:} IDEA provides fast framework, DICE identifies blockers, FATE validates resource availability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time investment:} 10-30 minutes\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
